From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 05:36:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Drop a vacuous test and restore the grant's ordering in the
 docs

The two_shards_dist replicas declare a <default_database>, so Cluster::Address
counts neither as local and the cluster has no local shard: the query endpoints
never asked that caller for CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE over it, before this change
or after, and the test asserting they do not was green either way. The two that
carry the property are the local-shard module, whose cluster entry has no
default database, and the restricted two-shard cluster user in the write module.

The docs sentence lost the ordering claim for CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE when it was
rewritten; the table-function path still checks it before the shard probe, so say
so again.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016i1XnLC3KypZdaYYsfzqub
---
 docs/concepts/features/interfaces/prometheus.mdx           | 2 +-
 .../test_prometheus_protocols/test_access_distributed.py   | 7 -------
 2 files changed, 1 insertion(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/concepts/features/interfaces/prometheus.mdx b/docs/concepts/features/interfaces/prometheus.mdx
index 85e1a882b5f..87932a17622 100644
--- a/docs/concepts/features/interfaces/prometheus.mdx
+++ b/docs/concepts/features/interfaces/prometheus.mdx
@@ -86,7 +86,7 @@ ClickHouse implements the Prometheus HTTP API over a [`TimeSeries`](/reference/e
 
 The query endpoints, remote write, and the `prometheusQuery`/`prometheusQueryRange` table functions also accept a `Distributed` table over per-shard `TimeSeries` tables: samples are selected on every shard and the PromQL is evaluated on the initiator, and writes are routed through the `Distributed` sink. Remote read and the metadata endpoints (`/api/v1/series`, `/api/v1/labels`, `/api/v1/label/<name>/values`, `/api/v1/metadata`) remain `TimeSeries`-only and refuse a `Distributed` target.
 
-Every shard-local table behind such a `Distributed` table must be a `TimeSeries` table declaring the same `time_series` type as the `Distributed` table itself; a mismatch is refused rather than rounded, and the check asks every replica of every shard on every request, so a shard-local table swapped for another under the same name is caught by the next request rather than read. The check asks each replica with `SHOW CREATE TABLE` and `DESC TABLE`, which need only `SHOW COLUMNS` on that table, a right any grant on it implies, so it reads nothing of `system` on the shard and a row policy or filter there cannot blind it; a replica that cannot answer at all is reported as unverified rather than failing the request. A replica that refuses to answer it, because the cluster's user holds only the column-level `INSERT` the write itself needs, is left to the check its own `INSERT` makes on the target it resolves, so the check never asks for a right the write does not. Remote write also sets `insert_expected_table_engine` to `TimeSeries` and `insert_expected_column_types` to the `time_series` type the table declares on the `INSERT` it dispatches, so a shard-local table swapped after that check, whether for another engine or for a `TimeSeries` table of another type, is refused where the `INSERT` resolves it on the shard, rather than taking the batch and having the sink convert the samples into it; the write then fails with a retryable `5xx`, Prometheus resends it, and the next check refuses it. A read is not failed by a replica the check cannot reach or that has no table of that name: whether the read itself fails is decided, as before, by its own `skip_unavailable_shards` settings. Remote write, however, is refused (with a retryable `5xx`) while any replica is unreachable, has no table of that name, or does not show that table's `time_series` type, because a replica the check could not see would still receive the samples unchecked. When the `Distributed` table declares no database, each shard resolves the name in its own default database, except a shard that is this server itself, which is always read and written in-process (`prefer_localhost_replica` is pinned on for both, and parallel replicas off for the reads) and so resolves it in the requesting user's current database, exactly as any query here would; the check resolves that shard's table in-process on the requesting user's context too, so the cluster's connection credentials play no part for it, and the write goes to the table so resolved, as any query keeps the tables it resolved even if a concurrent `RENAME` or `EXCHANGE` moves the name, so long as that table's database assigns UUIDs (`Atomic`, `Replicated`); where it does not, the write re-resolves the name and is refused, as on any other shard. Reading through it needs `SELECT` on the `Distributed` table and `READ ON REMOTE`, because the query is rewritten into a `cluster()` call; both are checked before the shard-local check above runs, so a caller without either is refused with a plain access error rather than told what the shards hold. The `prometheusQuery`/`prometheusQueryRange` table functions need `CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE` on top of those, as they do over a `TimeSeries` table, and that is the caller's own grant for naming a table function that is not readonly: the selector the rewrite names inside the `cluster()` call is readonly, so neither the cluster's user nor a caller of the query endpoints is ever asked for it. A shard that is this server itself is read in-process instead, so reading through such a cluster also needs `SELECT` on that shard's table, checked before the shard-local check runs too. Writing needs `INSERT` on the `Distributed` table and, whenever the cluster has a shard that is this server itself, on that shard's own table with the columns the sink sends, whether or not the batch routes any sample there; that one is checked before the shard-local check too. The `Distributed` table need not declare every outer column of a `TimeSeries` table: one declaring only `metric_name`, `tags` and `time_series` carries samples. It cannot carry metric metadata, though, because the sink sends exactly the columns the table declares, so a remote write that brings metadata is refused up front unless the table also declares `metric_family`, `type`, `unit` and `help`.
