base, each shard resolves the name in its own default database, except a shard that is this server itself, which is always read and written in-process (`prefer_localhost_replica` is pinned on for both, and parallel replicas off for the reads) and so resolves it in the requesting user's current database, exactly as any query here would; the check resolves that shard's table in-process on the requesting user's context too, so the cluster's connection credentials play no part for it, and the write goes to the table so resolved, as any query keeps the tables it resolved even if a concurrent `RENAME` or `EXCHANGE` moves the name, so long as that table's database assigns UUIDs (`Atomic`, `Replicated`); where it does not, the write re-resolves the name and is refused, as on any other shard. Reading through it needs `SELECT` on the `Distributed` table and `READ ON REMOTE`, because the query is rewritten into a `cluster()` call; both are checked before the shard-local check above runs, so a caller without either is refused with a plain access error rather than told what the shards hold. The `prometheusQuery`/`prometheusQueryRange` table functions need `CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE` on top of those, as they do over a `TimeSeries` table, and that is the caller's own grant for naming a table function that is not readonly, checked before the shard-local check runs; the selector the rewrite names inside the `cluster()` call is readonly, so neither the cluster's user nor a caller of the query endpoints is ever asked for it. A shard that is this server itself is read in-process instead, so reading through such a cluster also needs `SELECT` on that shard's table, checked before the shard-local check too. Writing needs `INSERT` on the `Distributed` table and, whenever the cluster has a shard that is this server itself, on that shard's own table with the columns the sink sends, whether or not the batch routes any sample there; that one is checked before the shard-local check too. The `Distributed` table need not declare every outer column of a `TimeSeries` table: one declaring only `metric_name`, `tags` and `time_series` carries samples. It cannot carry metric metadata, though, because the sink sends exactly the columns the table declares, so a remote write that brings metadata is refused up front unless the table also declares `metric_family`, `type`, `unit` and `help`.
 
 Remote write into a `Distributed` target is an `INSERT` through that table's sink with [`distributed_foreground_insert`](/reference/settings/session-settings/distributed#distributed_foreground_insert) forced on and [`async_insert`](/reference/settings/session-settings/async-insert#async_insert) forced off, whatever the handler's user profile or the `remote_write` URL says: the samples are not queued on the initiator, by the sink or by the asynchronous insert queue, but delivered to the shards before the response, so a `204` means every shard (one replica per shard under `internal_replication`) has accepted them, and a shard that fails the insert fails the write with a retryable `5xx`, which Prometheus answers by resending the batch. A batch left in either queue would be flushed after the check above, into whatever answers to the shard-local name by then, which is why background delivery is not offered on this path. [`skip_unavailable_shards`](/reference/settings/session-settings/skip-unavailable-shards#skip_unavailable_shards) is forced off on this path too, whatever the URL or the profile says: a shard the sink skipped would be samples dropped under a `204`. The routing settings `insert_shard_id` and `insert_distributed_one_random_shard` are refused on this path, whether they come from the URL or from the profile: a batch goes where the table's sharding key sends it, so a `Distributed` table with several shards and no key cannot be written through remote write.
 
